# =============================================================================
# .gitignore — what is committed vs. what stays local
# =============================================================================
# One rule decides everything in this file:
#
#     Commit what you AUTHOR. Ignore what you GENERATE.
#     Provenance travels with its artifact, in the store of record.
#
# Two consequences worth spelling out:
#
#   * Mutable => local. Anything a `make <stage>` run rewrites is derived
#     output; committing it produces pure churn (a new diff on every run)
#     with no value, because it can be regenerated from params.yaml.
#
#   * Data manifests are GENERATED, so they are local. A run's provenance is
#     already captured in MLflow: src/training/registry.py logs `manifest.json`
#     into the model's own run (as a `config/` artifact) and tags that run with
#     `manifest_sha256`. Since models/ and mlruns/ are local, the manifest that
#     describes them is local for exactly the same reason. Never commit a file
#     whose only consumer is another ignored artifact.
#
# The five classes:
#
#   1. Authored source ............... COMMITTED (everything not ignored below)
#                                     src/, tests/, .github/, params.yaml,
#                                     pyproject.toml, Makefile, Dockerfile*,
#                                     docker-compose.yml, README.md, .gitignore
#   2. Bootstrap templates ........... COMMITTED (small, hand-written, stable):
#                                     secrets/.env.example, *.gitkeep
#   3. Pipeline-generated output ..... IGNORED: data/, models/, reports/,
#                                     logs/, mlruns/, mlartifacts/
#   4. Secrets / local environment ... IGNORED
#   5. Local knowledge base .......... IGNORED by design (per project decision):
#                                     docs/ — see docs/stage1_review.md
# =============================================================================

# --- Caches & build artifacts (class 4) -------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/
.coverage

# --- Virtual environment (class 4) ------------------------------------------
.venv/
venv/

# --- Environment variables (class 4) ----------------------------------------
.env

# --- Secrets (class 4) ------------------------------------------------------
# Ignore the *contents* of secrets/ (not the directory itself) so the
# committed template below can be re-included. A directory-level `secrets/`
# would make the negation impossible — git never descends into an ignored dir.
secrets/*
!secrets/.env.example

# --- Backups (local safety copies — class 4) --------------------------------
backups/
data_backup_*/

# --- Pipeline-generated output: data (class 3) ------------------------------
# Everything under data/ is generated: `make ingest` writes the raw CSVs and
# their manifests, `make featurise` writes the processed features, the
# reference split and (WS0) the superset in its own subdirectory. A single
# `**` rule covers every nesting level — the raw caches
# (data/raw/entsoe/generation/, data/raw/weather/) and the WS0 superset
# directory (data/processed/superset/) alike — so no future artifact can leak
# into git the way a per-extension rule would allow.
#
# Ignore the *contents* of data/ (not the directory itself) so the three
# committed *.gitkeep placeholders can be re-included below: a
# directory-level `data/` rule would make those negations impossible, exactly
# the trap already documented for secrets/.
data/**
!data/**/
!data/**/.gitkeep

# --- Pipeline-generated output: models / reports / logs / MLflow (class 3) ---
# `models/*` + `!models/.gitkeep` keeps the placeholder but drops the artifacts.
models/*
!models/.gitkeep

reports/*
!reports/.gitkeep

logs/

mlruns/
mlartifacts/
.mlflow/
*.db

# --- Local knowledge base (class 5, ignored by design) ----------------------
docs/

# --- IDE / OS / Docker (class 4) --------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo

.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

.docker/
